To perform a binary search on a large dataset of strings in C++, you can use the `std::binary_search` function from the `<algorithm>` header. Here's an example code snippet demonstrating the necessary steps:
```c++
#include <iostream>
#include <algorithm>
#include <vector>
#include <string>
int main() {
    // Create a vector of strings to search through
    std::vector<std::string> strings = {"apple", "banana", "cherry", "date", "elderberry", "fig", "grape"};
    // Sort the vector in alphabetical order
    std::sort(strings.begin(), strings.end());
    // Perform a binary search for the string "cherry"
    std::string search_string = "cherry";
    bool found = std::binary_search(strings.begin(), strings.end(), search_string);
    // Print the result of the search
    if (found) {
        std::cout << "The string \"" << search_string << "\" was found." << std::endl;
    } else {
        std::cout << "The string \"" << search_string << "\" was not found." << std::endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this example, we first create a vector of strings to search through. We then sort the vector in alphabetical order using the `std::sort` function. Finally, we perform a binary search for the string "cherry" using the `std::binary_search` function and print the result of the search.

