Multithreading is a way to improve the efficiency of a program by allowing it to execute multiple tasks simultaneously. In Java, multithreading can be implemented by creating multiple threads of execution within a single program.
To create a new thread in Java, you can either extend the Thread class or implement the Runnable interface. Here's an example of how to create a new thread using the Runnable interface:
```
public class MyRunnable implements Runnable {
    public void run() {
        // Code to be executed in this thread
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        MyRunnable myRunnable = new MyRunnable();
        Thread thread = new Thread(myRunnable);
        thread.start();
    }
}
```
In this example, we define a new class `MyRunnable` that implements the `Runnable` interface. The `run()` method in this class contains the code that will be executed in the new thread. In the `Main` class, we create a new instance of `MyRunnable` and pass it to a new `Thread` object. Finally, we call the `start()` method on the thread to begin execution.
Multithreading can be particularly beneficial in scenarios where there are tasks that can be executed independently of each other. For example, in a web server, each incoming request can be handled in a separate thread. This allows the server to handle multiple requests simultaneously, improving its overall performance.
Another example is in a video game, where the game logic can be executed in a separate thread from the graphics rendering. This allows the game to maintain a consistent frame rate even if the game logic is complex and takes a long time to execute.
In addition to these examples, there are many creative ways to use multithreading to optimize program performance. For example, in a data processing application, you could split the data into multiple chunks and process each chunk in a separate thread. This would allow the processing to be done in parallel, reducing the overall processing time.
Overall, multithreading is a powerful tool for improving the efficiency of a program, and there are many scenarios where it can be used to great effect.

