One way to retrieve the prime numbers from a list of integers in Java is by implementing the Sieve of Eratosthenes algorithm. This algorithm works by creating a boolean array of all numbers up to a given limit, initially setting all values to true. Then, starting from the first prime number (2), all multiples of that number are set to false. The next prime number is then found by searching for the next true value in the array, and the process is repeated until all numbers up to the limit have been checked.
Here's a code snippet that implements this algorithm:
```
public static List<Integer> getPrimes(List<Integer> numbers) {
    List<Integer> primes = new ArrayList<>();
    boolean[] isPrime = new boolean[Collections.max(numbers) + 1];
    Arrays.fill(isPrime, true);
    isPrime[0] = false;
    isPrime[1] = false;
    for (int i = 2; i <= Math.sqrt(Collections.max(numbers)); i++) {
        if (isPrime[i]) {
            for (int j = i * i; j <= Collections.max(numbers); j += i) {
                isPrime[j] = false;
            }
        }
    }
    for (int number : numbers) {
        if (isPrime[number]) {
            primes.add(number);
        }
    }
    return primes;
}
