What is the difference between procedural and object-oriented programming in PHP? Can you provide an example of a task that is easier to accomplish in an object-oriented approach compared to a procedural approach in PHP?
In procedural programming, code is organized around procedures or functions, while in object-oriented programming, code is organized around objects, which encapsulate data and behavior. For example, if you wanted to create a program that manages a list of employees, it may be easier to do so using an object-oriented approach, where each employee is represented as an object with its own properties and methods, rather than a procedural approach where you would need to manage arrays or other data structures manually.
Here is an example of how to define a simple class in PHP:
<?php
class Employee {
  public $name;
  public $id;

  function __construct($name, $id) {
    $this->name = $name;
    $this->id = $id;
  }

  function getDetails() {
    return "Employee name: " . $this->name . ", ID: " . $this->id;
  }
}
$employee1 = new Employee("John Doe", 1234);
echo $employee1->getDetails();
?>
This will output: "Employee name: John Doe, ID: 1234". In this example, we have defined a class called Employee with properties for name and ID, as well as a constructor method to set these properties when an object is created. We have also defined a method called getDetails that returns a formatted string with the employee's name and ID. By using an object-oriented approach, we can easily create and manage multiple instances of the Employee class, each with their own unique data and behavior.

