While this approach can work for simple tasks like managing a list of employees, it can become more difficult to maintain and extend as the program grows in complexity. For example, if we wanted to add new properties or methods to our employee objects, we would need to modify our procedural code to handle these changes. In contrast, with an object-oriented approach, we can simply add new properties or methods to our Employee class, and any code that uses this class will automatically have access to these changes.

To optimize the time complexity of the program, we can use the fact that a number n is prime if and only if it is not divisible by any prime number less than or equal to the square root of n. 
Therefore, we can iterate over all prime numbers less than or equal to the square root of n, and check if any of them divide n. If none of them divide n, then n is prime.
Here is the optimized program in Python:
```
import math
def is_prime(n):
    if n <= 1:
        return False
    elif n <= 3:
        return True
    elif n % 2 == 0 or n % 3 == 0:
        return False
    else:
        for i in range(5, int(math.sqrt(n)) + 1, 6):
            if n % i == 0 or n % (i + 2) == 0:
                return False
        return True
n = int(input())
if is_prime(n):
    print("Yes, the number is prime")
else:
    print("No, the number is not prime")
```
This program has a time complexity of O(sqrt(n)), which is much faster than iterating over all numbers up to n. It should be able to handle numbers up to 10^9 within the given time limit of 5 seconds.

To implement the First Law of Thermodynamics in a C++ program, you can use the following formula:
ΔU = Q - W
where ΔU is the change in internal energy of the system, Q is the heat transferred to the system, and W is the work done by the system.
Here is an example C++ code that calculates the internal energy, heat, and work variables in a thermodynamic system:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    double Q, W, U;
    double m, c, T1, T2;
    cout << "Enter the mass of the system (in kg): ";
    cin >> m;
    cout << "Enter the specific heat capacity of the system (in J/kg.K): ";
    cin >> c;
    cout << "Enter the initial temperature of the system (in K): ";
    cin >> T1;
    cout << "Enter the final temperature of the system (in K): ";
    cin >> T2;
    Q = m * c * (T2 - T1);
    W = 0; // assume no work is done
    U = Q - W;
    cout << "The heat transferred to the system is: " << Q << " J" << endl;
    cout << "The work done by the system is: " << W << " J" << endl;
    cout << "The change in internal energy of the system is: " << U << " J" << endl;
    return 0;
}
```
To relate the concept of entropy to the Second Law of Thermodynamics, we can use the following formula:
ΔS = Q/T
where ΔS is the change in entropy of the system, Q is the heat transferred to the system, and T is the temperature of the system.
This formula states that the entropy of a closed system will always increase over time, unless energy is added to the system from an external source. This is because the Second Law of Thermodynamics states that the total entropy of a closed system will always increase over time.
At absolute zero, the entropy of a perfect crystal is zero, according to the Third Law of Thermodynamics. This is because at absolute zero, the atoms in the crystal stop moving, and there is no randomness or disorder in the system.
Here is an example C++ code that calculates the entropy of a system:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <cmath>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    double Q, T, S;
    cout << "Enter the heat transferred to the system (in J): ";
    cin >> Q;
    cout << "Enter the temperature of the system (in K): ";
    cin >> T;
    S = Q/T;
    cout << "The change in entropy of the system is: " << S << " J/K" << endl;
    return 0;
}
```

